  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ where $f(x)=x^2$. Prove that $f$ is convex on $\\mathbb R$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f$ is convex on $\\mathbb R$.\n\nFor $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ and $0\\le t\\le1$, use $2ab\\le a^2+b^2$ to obtain $f(ta+(1-t)b)\\le tf(a)+(1-t)f(b)$.",
      "worked": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ and $0\\le t\\le1$. Since $(a-b)^2\\ge0$, we have $2ab\\le a^2+b^2$. Also, $t(1-t)\\ge0$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&=(ta+(1-t)b)^2\\\\\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&=t^2a^2+(1-2t+t^2)b^2+2abt(1-t)\\\\\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&\\le t^2a^2+(1-2t+t^2)b^2+(a^2+b^2)t(1-t)\\\\\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&\\le t^2a^2+(1-2t+t^2)b^2+ta^2-t^2a^2+tb^2-t^2b^2\\\\\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&\\le ta^2+b^2-tb^2\\\\\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&\\le ta^2+(1-t)b^2\\\\\nf(ta+(1-t)b)&\\le tf(a)+(1-t)f(b).\n\\end{align*}$$\nThis is the defining inequality for convexity, so $f$ is convex on $\\mathbb R$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
